#[
    Block Recursive Locals Example
]#

#[
    A block resolves locals against the frame that wrote 
    it, and when the same method is on the stack several 
    times, that means the "right" one, not merely one of 
    them.

    So `walk(n)` binds its own `mine`, then recurses 
    passing a "new literal block" closing over that 
    frame's `mine`. No block forwarding is needed, each 
    level supplies its own, so while `walk(2)` is 
    executing, the block it yields to belongs to `walk(3)`.
]#

define walk(n)
    mine = n * 100

    if n <= 0
        return 0
    end

    got = yield n
    
    deeper = walk(n - 1) do |x|
        mine + x
    end
    return got + deeper
end

echo walk(3) do |x|
    x
end
